#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""adapters/codex/run -- see README.md (this directory) for the two
configuration keys, and ../README.md for the shared adapter contract.

The capability read (issue #41, recorded on the issue 2026-09-08 against
codex-cli 0.153.4): `codex exec review --output-schema <FILE>` ignores
the schema entirely -- confirmed live against a real planted-defect
diff, its `-o` output is always free-text prose, never JSON, whatever
schema is named. This adapter therefore never invokes `codex exec
review`; both stages feed their own instruction to plain `codex exec -`
on stdin (confirmed live: this path does honor --output-schema, and
`-p` is codex's `--profile`, not a prompt flag, so stdin is the only
channel and it is a positional `-`, not a flag).

Two channels, not one. `--json` prints events to stdout as JSONL
(thread.started, turn.started, item.*, and a terminal turn.completed or
turn.failed carrying usage) -- read for the model's own account of
usage and for the terminal event; codex's own exit code is 1 on
turn.failed, 0 on turn.completed, read directly, so this adapter reads
the terminal event for the reason, not for the verdict. The structured
answer comes from `--output-schema <FILE>` plus `--output-last-message
<FILE>`, written as one JSON object once the turn completes -- that is
where findings and tests_named come from; parsing them out of the event
stream would be the wrong shape.

No model id appears anywhere codex reports -- not the event stream, not
`codex doctor`, not any config file (confirmed live 2026-09-08; there is
no ~/.codex/config.toml on this machine at all). [executor.codex].model
is therefore required, not optional: this adapter refuses before ever
invoking codex if it is absent, and reports back exactly the id it was
given, never one derived from output.

auth mirrors claude-code's auth = "subscription": the operator's own
machine is logged in via a ChatGPT plan, and an OPENAI_API_KEY left in
the environment would silently switch codex to API billing on a run
meant for the plan. [executor.codex].auth names the mode this
invocation is trusted to run under, checked against OPENAI_API_KEY
alone (deterministic and testable without a live `codex doctor` call,
which a canned-response or faked-tool_binary test situation cannot
answer honestly anyway) -- this adapter refuses rather than silently
falling back when they disagree, because a wrong credential here is a
bill, not just a wrong answer. cost_usd is therefore always null on
this adapter (no dollar figure appears anywhere under plan auth, and
none is invented for API auth either): a codex record carrying a
non-null cost would be evidence the wrong credential was used, a
finding about the credential, not a measurement.

Not exercised live by the test suite (judgment is measured, not
tested); its argument construction is checked by ATTAVO_ADAPTER_DRY_RUN
(prints {"argv", "instruction", "schema"} instead of running -- argv
alone would carry no instruction, since it goes in on stdin), and its
response handling is checked by ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE, which
feeds a canned {"events": [...JSONL objects...], "final": {...the
output-file's parsed content, or null...}, "exit_code": N} instead of
invoking codex -- codex's own canned-response hook, since codex itself
has none (issue #41). A JSON array of such objects is a queue, one per
invocation this adapter makes, its last element repeating once
exhausted; a single object still repeats for every call (issue #88: the
non-question retry below is a second invocation inside the same run).

Issue #88 (class A: a report is not a question). Exactly claude-code's
own fix, against the same build-side status/question channel: a
"question" whose text clears MIN_QUESTION_CHARS but carries no "?" is a
report, not a question, and this adapter re-invokes codex once more
(REINSTRUCTION appended to the same instruction) before ever reporting a
halt. A second report in a row still halts -- someone must look -- but
its report line carries "class": "non-question" and both raw results
already reached stdout above it.

Issue #88 (class B: work that outlives the invocation). `codex exec`'s
own surface -- `codex exec --help` (codex-cli 0.153.4), the JSONL event
stream (thread.*, turn.*, item.*), and the --output-schema/
--output-last-message response -- names no subagent or background-task
concept at all: codex is a single agent driving sandboxed shell
commands, with no tool of its own that could spawn work outliving this
invocation the way claude-code's Agent/Workflow tools did (issue #88,
confirmed live on claude-code, this adapter's own conformance.json
declares codex's guard unsupported for exactly this reason rather than
asserting a check against a concept that is not there).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

# the helper beside adapters/, imported without leaving a __pycache__ there
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent))
from executor_env import executor_env  # noqa: E402

STATUS_SCHEMA = {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": False,
    "required": ["status", "question"],
    "properties": {
        "status": {"type": "string", "enum": ["done", "question"]},
        "question": {"type": ["string", "null"]},
    },
}

VERIFY_SCHEMA = {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": False,
    "required": ["findings", "tests_named"],
    "properties": {
        "findings": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
                "type": "object",
                "additionalProperties": False,
                "required": ["title", "specimen", "retires", "rejection_test", "severity"],
                "properties": {
                    "title": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                    "specimen": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                    "retires": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                    "rejection_test": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                    "severity": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                },
            },
        },
        "tests_named": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1}},
    },
}

# The counsel objective (issue #100): a stop is read inside the product
# first, before a person. Identical to claude-code's own COUNSEL_SCHEMA --
# the counsel structured answer is the shared adapter contract, not
# either tool's own shape.
COUNSEL_SCHEMA = {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": False,
    "required": ["class", "verdict", "citations", "proposal"],
    "properties": {
        "class": {"type": "string"},
        "verdict": {"type": "string", "enum": ["answer", "patch", "person"]},
        "citations": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
                "type": "object",
                "additionalProperties": False,
                "required": ["kind", "path", "symbol", "ruling_id"],
                "properties": {
                    "kind": {"type": "string", "enum": ["instruction", "mechanism", "guard"]},
                    "path": {"type": ["string", "null"]},
                    "symbol": {"type": ["string", "null"]},
                    "ruling_id": {"type": ["string", "null"]},
                },
            },
        },
        "proposal": {
            "type": "object",
            "additionalProperties": False,
            "required": ["ruling_text", "patch", "question", "issue"],
            "properties": {
                "ruling_text": {"type": ["string", "null"]},
                "patch": {
                    "type": ["object", "null"],
                    "additionalProperties": False,
                    "required": ["file", "symbol", "rejection_test", "diff"],
                    "properties": {
                        "file": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                        "symbol": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                        "rejection_test": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                        "diff": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                    },
                },
                "question": {"type": ["string", "null"]},
                # issue #175: the fourth proposal shape, for a "patch" or
                # "person" verdict whose remedy is better filed as an item
                # than shipped as a diff or asked as a bare question.
                "issue": {
                    "type": ["object", "null"],
                    "additionalProperties": False,
                    "required": ["title", "body"],
                    "properties": {
                        "title": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                        "body": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                    },
                },
            },
        },
    },
}


def read_text(path_env: str) -> str:
    path = os.environ.get(path_env)
    if not path:
        return ""
    p = Path(path)
    return p.read_text() if p.is_file() else ""


def describe_file(path: str, text: str, diff_like: bool) -> str:
    """Path, byte count, line count, and (for a diff) files touched --
    named in the instruction instead of inlining the file's own text, so
    the instruction stays a small fixed size whatever the file's size
    (issue #86: a 27,367-line deletion diff inlined whole exceeded
    codex's own input ceiling). Files touched counts unified-diff file
    headers (`diff --git a/... b/...`), the shape `git diff` produces."""
    size = Path(path).stat().st_size
    lines = text.count("\n") + (1 if text and not text.endswith("\n") else 0)
    if not diff_like:
        return f"`{path}` ({size} bytes, {lines} lines)"
    files = sum(1 for l in text.splitlines() if l.startswith("diff --git "))
    return f"`{path}` ({size} bytes, {lines} lines, {files} files touched)"


def spec_sections() -> list[str]:
    """The spec's Problem, Claims, and Criteria sections verbatim -- the
    same contract adapters/claude-code/run reads, since what a verifier
    must be told is a product fact, not a tool-specific one."""
    spec_dir = Path(os.environ["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"])
    lines = (spec_dir / "spec.md").read_text().splitlines()
    idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith("## ")]
    headings = {lines[i].strip(): i for i in idx}
    bounds = sorted(idx) + [len(lines)]

    def section(heading: str) -> str:
        start = headings[heading]
        end = bounds[bounds.index(start) + 1] if start in bounds else len(lines)
        return "\n".join(lines[start:end]).strip()

    return [section("## Problem"), section("## Claims"), section("## Criteria")]


def stub_note() -> str | None:
    """None when ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR carries no stub/; else the sentence
    naming what a stub is (issue #90, closed by #93). The stub is
    evidence -- a criterion it satisfies trivially is weak -- but twice
    the judge reported
    a built tree as failing to match its stub as if the stub were the
    intended change (tenant zero #664, #644): one sentence of vocabulary
    was missing, not a smarter judge or a dropped stub."""
    stub_dir = Path(os.environ["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"]) / "stub"
    if not stub_dir.is_dir():
        return None
    return (
        f"## The stub at `{stub_dir}`\n\n"
        "The stub is the fixture the acceptance criteria were shown to "
        "pass against; it is not the intended change. A difference "
        "between the built tree and the stub is not a finding: a "
        "criterion the stub satisfies trivially is.")


def build_instruction() -> str:
    parts = list(spec_sections())

    rulings = read_text("ATTAVO_RULINGS")
    if rulings.strip():
        parts.append("## Rulings\n\n" + rulings.strip())

    feedback_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_FEEDBACK")
    feedback = read_text("ATTAVO_FEEDBACK")
    if feedback.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## Feedback from the previous attempt\n\n"
            f"The feedback is at {describe_file(feedback_path, feedback, diff_like=False)}. "
            "It is not inlined here: read it yourself with your own tools "
            "before continuing.")

    parts.append(
        "commit your work on the current branch; if you cannot proceed "
        "without a decision that changes the criteria, do not guess: "
        "answer with status question")

    test_command = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_TEST_COMMAND")
    if test_command:
        bound = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_TEST_TIMEOUT_S")
        parts.append(
            f"to run tests while you work, run `{test_command}`: it runs the tests "
            "selected for the files you have changed"
            + (f" and stops at {bound} s" if bound else "")
            + ". Run a single test with `" + test_command + " <path or test id>`. "
            "Do not run the test runner directly, the whole test suite, or any "
            "whole-tree check yourself: when the criteria and " + "`" + test_command + "`"
            + " pass, commit and stop; the gates are run for you and any failure is "
            "returned to you on the next iteration")

    return "\n\n".join(parts)


def verify_instruction() -> str:
    parts = list(spec_sections())

    note = stub_note()
    if note:
        parts.append(note)

    diff_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_DIFF")
    diff = read_text("ATTAVO_DIFF")
    if diff.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## Diff since the approved spec\n\n"
            f"The diff is at {describe_file(diff_path, diff, diff_like=True)}. "
            "It is not inlined here: read it yourself with your own tools "
            "before judging it.")
    else:
        parts.append("## Diff since the approved spec\n\n(the diff is empty)")

    build_record_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD")
    build_record = read_text("ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD")
    if build_record.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The build record\n\n"
            f"The build record is at {describe_file(build_record_path, build_record, diff_like=False)}. "
            "Read it yourself with your own tools.")

    parts.append(
        "you are verifying a completed build, not building: make no edits, "
        "write no files, commit nothing. Read the diff named above against "
        "the criteria and name what the criteria do not assert but the diff "
        "should still be judged on, and name the tests that would assert "
        "it if they existed. A spec whose approval commit does not "
        "describe the working tree the build produced is itself a "
        "finding: the diff is judged against what was approved, and a "
        "divergence between them is reportable rather than assumed "
        "away. Report each as a finding: title, specimen (the file or "
        "commit the finding is about), retires (what having this "
        "finding on record replaces or closes -- every finding must "
        "carry one, or it is refused unfiled), rejection_test (the test "
        "that would catch a regression of it), and severity. Severity "
        "is derived rather than judged: the version, counter, "
        "condition, or state change that flips a finding from harmless "
        "to blocking; an adjective is not a severity. An empty "
        "findings list means you looked and found nothing to report, not "
        "that you did not look.")
    return "\n\n".join(parts)


def counsel_instruction() -> str:
    """Issue #100 (extended by issue #175): a stop is read inside the
    product first, before a person. Identical to claude-code's own
    counsel_instruction -- the record, its log, the diff, the forge's
    checks, the item's graph, the tenant manifest, and this product's own
    root are all handed by path, named rather than inlined, the same
    discipline verify_instruction already applies to the diff (issue
    #86)."""
    parts = list(spec_sections())

    rulings = read_text("ATTAVO_RULINGS")
    if rulings.strip():
        parts.append("## Rulings\n\n" + rulings.strip())

    note = stub_note()
    if note:
        parts.append(note)

    record_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD")
    record_text = read_text("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD")
    if record_text.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The stage record\n\n"
            f"The record is at {describe_file(record_path, record_text, diff_like=False)}. "
            "Read it yourself with your own tools.")

    log_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG")
    log_text = read_text("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG")
    if log_text.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The record's log\n\n"
            f"The log is at {describe_file(log_path, log_text, diff_like=False)}. "
            "Read it yourself with your own tools.")

    diff_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF")
    diff_text = read_text("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF")
    if diff_text.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The item's diff since approval\n\n"
            f"The diff is at {describe_file(diff_path, diff_text, diff_like=True)}. "
            "Read it yourself with your own tools.")

    # issue #175: the forge in the evidence set. ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS is
    # always written, a JSON {read, reason, checks} envelope -- read false
    # names the missing manifest key rather than leaving the section out,
    # since an unread forge must never be mistaken for a green one.
    checks_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS")
    checks_text = read_text("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS")
    if checks_text.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The forge's checks on this stop's sha\n\n"
            f"The checks are at {describe_file(checks_path, checks_text, diff_like=False)}: "
            "a JSON object -- read: false means the forge was not read at "
            "all (reason names the missing manifest key; treat this as "
            "unknown, never as green) -- read: true carries one entry per "
            "check, each naming, for a check that did not succeed, the "
            "path of its own failed-step log. Read the failing check's "
            "log before answering about the forge; a remedy that depends "
            "on the forge's state cites the check and its log line.")

    # issue #175: the graph in the evidence set -- the default branch's
    # own log since this item's branch point, this branch's own commits,
    # the markers and records present at the stop's sha, and the check
    # runs on the last three shas, so "the checks are on the record
    # commit, not the code commit" is readable without a terminal.
    graph_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_GRAPH")
    graph_text = read_text("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_GRAPH")
    if graph_text.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The item's graph\n\n"
            f"The graph is at {describe_file(graph_path, graph_text, diff_like=False)}. "
            "Read it yourself with your own tools.")

    manifest_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_MANIFEST", "")
    parts.append(f"## The tenant manifest\n\nThe manifest is at `{manifest_path}`. "
                 "Read it yourself with your own tools.")

    product_root = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_PRODUCT_ROOT", "")
    parts.append(
        "## The product's own root\n\n"
        f"The product that ran this stage is at `{product_root}` "
        "(`bin/attavo`, `adapters/`, `schema/`, `templates/`). A stop "
        "whose cause is the product's own mechanism, not the tenant's, "
        "is read here.")

    # issue #175: attavo counsel N --ask hands the operator's own text as
    # the question to answer, over the same evidence set, rather than
    # diagnosing a stop -- the record above is context, not necessarily a
    # failure.
    question = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_COUNSEL_QUESTION", "")
    if question.strip():
        parts.append(
            "## The operator's question\n\n" + question.strip() +
            "\n\nAnswer this question using the evidence above; the "
            "record named above is context, not necessarily a failure.")

    parts.append(
        "you are counseling a stop, read-only: make no edits, write no "
        "files, commit nothing. Read the record, its log, the spec, the "
        "rulings, the diff, the forge's checks, the item's graph, the "
        "tenant manifest, and the product's own root named above with "
        "your own tools, then answer with exactly one class (the stop's "
        "class, or \"unclassed\"), one verdict -- \"answer\" (the stop is "
        "answerable inside the rules: a ruling text, in "
        "proposal.ruling_text, with three citations -- instruction, "
        "mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id), "
        "\"patch\" (the stop is a product defect: the mechanism's file "
        "and symbol, a rejection test, and a unified diff, in "
        "proposal.patch -- or, when the remedy is better filed as an "
        "item than shipped as a diff, a title and a body in this "
        "repository's own issue format -- a Severity/Provenance line, "
        "then Problem, Retires, Change, and Acceptance sections -- in "
        "proposal.issue), or \"person\" (the answer would touch the "
        "definition of done and no rule covers it: the question restated "
        "in one line, in proposal.question -- or, when the remedy is "
        "better filed as an item than asked as a bare question, a title "
        "and a body in the same issue format, in proposal.issue) -- and "
        "citations for whichever verdict you gave. You propose; you do "
        "not act.")
    return "\n\n".join(parts)


def load_config() -> dict:
    raw = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG")
    return json.loads(raw) if raw else {}


# codex's own ceiling (issue #93): "Input exceeds the maximum length of
# 1048576 characters" -- confirmed live 2026-09-08 against codex-cli
# 0.153.4, the same specimen issue #86 fixed by handing the diff over by
# path (tenant zero #659, verify-01: input_too_large, max_chars 1048576,
# actual_chars 1297762). Declared here and in conformance.json's own
# input_limit so a test can assert the two agree.
INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS = 1_048_576


def input_limit_chars() -> int:
    """The declared ceiling, chars. Overridable only for tests via
    ATTAVO_ADAPTER_INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS -- never a tenant knob: the ceiling
    is a fact about codex, not something [executor.codex] can set (a
    tenant typing a number it does not know is the pattern rule 42
    refuses)."""
    override = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ADAPTER_INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS")
    return int(override) if override else INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS


def ceiling_refusal(instruction: str) -> str | None:
    """None if instruction stays at or under codex's declared ceiling;
    else the refusal naming both numbers, checked before codex is ever
    invoked -- so an oversized prompt never reaches the tool that would
    refuse it anyway, three seconds and a wasted call later."""
    limit = input_limit_chars()
    size = len(instruction)
    if size <= limit:
        return None
    return (f"ceiling exceeded -- instruction is {size} chars, over "
           f"codex's declared limit of {limit} chars")


def build_argv(schema_path: Path, output_path: Path, config: dict, stage: str) -> list[str]:
    # verify and counsel (issue #100) are mechanically read-only, not
    # just instructed to be: the sandbox enforces "make no edits, write
    # no files, commit nothing" rather than trusting the instruction
    # alone. Any stage other than "build" gets the read-only sandbox.
    sandbox = "workspace-write" if stage == "build" else "read-only"
    return ["codex", "exec", "-",
           "--model", config["model"],
           "--sandbox", sandbox,
           "--output-schema", str(schema_path),
           "--output-last-message", str(output_path),
           "--json",
           "--ignore-user-config", "--ignore-rules"]


def check_auth(config: dict) -> str | None:
    """None if the declared auth mode and OPENAI_API_KEY's presence
    agree; else the mismatch, refused before codex is ever invoked."""
    auth = config.get("auth", "subscription")
    has_key = bool(os.environ.get("OPENAI_API_KEY"))
    if auth == "subscription" and has_key:
        return ("auth is \"subscription\" but OPENAI_API_KEY is set in the "
                "environment -- codex would silently bill the API account "
                "instead of running under the plan")
    if auth == "api" and not has_key:
        return "auth is \"api\" but OPENAI_API_KEY is not set in the environment"
    return None


def parse_events(stdout: str) -> list[dict]:
    """One JSON object per JSONL line; a tracing line (e.g. "ERROR
    codex_api::...") is not JSON and is skipped, not an error -- codex
    writes both to the same stream under --json."""
    events = []
    for line in stdout.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            events.append(json.loads(line))
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return events


def terminal_event(events: list[dict]) -> dict | None:
    for e in events:
        if e.get("type") in ("turn.completed", "turn.failed"):
            return e
    return None


EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS = 400


def append_event(events_path: str, kind: str, text: str) -> None:
    """One {at, kind, text} JSON line appended to events_path and
    flushed at once -- the same shape and the same open-write-close
    discipline bin/attavo's own append_event and claude-code's own copy
    use, kept independently here since an adapter is a separate process
    with no import path into the product or into another adapter."""
    line = {"at": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
           "kind": kind, "text": " ".join(text.split())[:EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS]}
    with open(events_path, "a") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(line) + "\n")


def first_line(text: str) -> str:
    stripped = text.strip()
    return stripped.splitlines()[0] if stripped else ""


def item_events(obj: dict) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """[(kind, text), ...] for one parsed codex --json stream line --
    zero, one, or two (a command_execution item carries both the
    command and, once it has run, its own output, so it maps to a
    tool_call and a tool_result together). An agent_message item's own
    text maps to "message" -- confirmed live (issue #41, recorded
    2026-09-08 against codex-cli 0.153.4): conformance.json's own
    "done" situation, `{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"type":
    "agent_message", "text": "ok"}}`, is this exact shape. A
    command_execution item's "command" maps to "tool_call" (the
    command's own first line) and its "aggregated_output", once
    present, to "tool_result" (the output's own first line) -- both
    field names are confirmed, by tenant zero's own verify logs on
    attavo/code-factory since #649: every command_execution item there
    carries exactly the keys aggregated_output, command, exit_code, id,
    status, type, the same shape tests/test_adapter_codex.py's recorded
    fixture exercises this mapping through. Every other item type
    (reasoning, file_change, mcp_tool_call, and the rest), and every
    non-item.completed line (thread.*, turn.*, "result" itself), maps
    to nothing here."""
    if obj.get("type") != "item.completed":
        return []
    item = obj.get("item") or {}
    kind = item.get("type")
    events: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    if kind == "agent_message" and item.get("text"):
        events.append(("message", first_line(item["text"])))
    elif kind == "command_execution":
        command = item.get("command")
        if command:
            events.append(("tool_call", first_line(str(command))))
        output = item.get("aggregated_output")
        if output:
            events.append(("tool_result", first_line(str(output))))
    return events


def write_events(events_path: str, parsed_events: list[dict]) -> None:
    for obj in parsed_events:
        for kind, text in item_events(obj):
            append_event(events_path, kind, text)


def load_canned_responses() -> list[dict] | None:
    """None when ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE is unset. Otherwise the
    queue of canned {"events", "final", "exit_code"} objects this run
    answers from: a single object repeats for every invocation this
    adapter makes (unchanged since v0.3.0 -- most tests make exactly
    one), a JSON array is consumed in order, one response per
    invocation, its last element repeating once exhausted (issue #88:
    the non-question retry is a second invocation inside the same
    adapter run, and a canned test needs to hand it a different answer
    than the first)."""
    response_file = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE")
    if not response_file:
        return None
    raw = json.loads(Path(response_file).read_text())
    return raw if isinstance(raw, list) else [raw]


def invoke(instruction: str, schema: dict, config: dict, stage: str,
          responses: list[dict] | None, call_index: int
          ) -> tuple[str, list[dict], dict | None, int]:
    """(raw transcript text, parsed events, the output file's parsed
    content or None, exit code) -- from responses[call_index] (clamped
    to the last element) when responses is not None, else a real
    `codex exec -` invocation."""
    if responses is not None:
        canned = responses[min(call_index, len(responses) - 1)]
        events = canned.get("events") or []
        final = canned.get("final")
        exit_code = canned.get("exit_code", 0)
        raw = "\n".join(json.dumps(e) for e in events)
        return raw, events, final, exit_code

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        schema_path = Path(tmp) / "schema.json"
        schema_path.write_text(json.dumps(schema))
        output_path = Path(tmp) / "output.json"

        argv = build_argv(schema_path, output_path, config, stage)
        run = subprocess.run(argv, input=instruction, capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=False, env=executor_env())
        raw = run.stdout
        if run.returncode != 0 and run.stderr.strip():
            raw = (raw + "\n" if raw else "") + run.stderr.rstrip("\n")

        final = None
        if output_path.is_file():
            try:
                final = json.loads(output_path.read_text())
            except ValueError:
                final = None
        return raw, parse_events(run.stdout), final, run.returncode


def adapter_version() -> str:
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["codex", "--version"], capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=False)
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return out.stdout.strip()


MIN_QUESTION_CHARS = 40

REINSTRUCTION = (
    "your last answer was a report, not a question; either continue to "
    "done, or ask one question ending in a question mark that names the "
    "spec line or guard you cannot resolve")


def question_class(status: str, question: str | None) -> str:
    """"done" (no halt at all), "fail" (a question too short or absent to
    be worth a person's judgment -- MIN_QUESTION_CHARS, unchanged since
    v0.15.0), "report" (long enough but carries no "?" -- issue #88's
    class A, a status update in the question channel), or "question" (a
    real question). Identical to claude-code's own -- the build-side
    status/question channel is the shared adapter contract, not either
    tool's own shape."""
    if status != "question":
        return "done"
    if not question or len(question) < MIN_QUESTION_CHARS:
        # An absent or too-short question is a report with nothing in
        # it: a status update in the question channel (issue #150, five
        # specimens on 2026-09-17), never an exit with no report line.
        return "report"
    if "?" not in question:
        return "report"
    return "question"


# R3: a vendor's capacity, rate, or 5xx refusal is transient -- classed
# on this adapter's own report line, the same way non-question already
# is (issue #88), so a pass retries it plainly instead of paying for a
# counsel run on a stop the definition of done never touches.
TRANSIENT_MARKERS = ("overloaded", "rate limit", "rate_limit", "429", "500", "502", "503", "529",
                    "internal server error", "service unavailable", "temporarily unavailable")


def transient_detail(events: list[dict]) -> str | None:
    """The terminal turn.failed event's own message when it names one of
    TRANSIENT_MARKERS; else None -- a turn.failed for any other reason
    (an auth mismatch, a malformed request) is not this."""
    term = terminal_event(events)
    if term is None or term.get("type") != "turn.failed":
        return None
    message = str((term.get("error") or {}).get("message") or "")
    low = message.lower()
    return message if any(marker in low for marker in TRANSIENT_MARKERS) else None


def usage_fields(events: list[dict]) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """(tokens.input, tokens.output) for one call's own event stream --
    split out from report_fields so a retry's first, discarded call
    still counts (issue #88: a non-question costs two executor turns,
    and a report line built from the second call alone undercounts by
    exactly the first one)."""
    term = terminal_event(events)
    usage = (term or {}).get("usage") or {}
    return usage.get("input_tokens", 0) or 0, usage.get("output_tokens", 0) or 0


def report_fields(model: str, events: list[dict]) -> dict:
    """The fields common to every final report line, derived from one
    call's own event stream -- model is always the configured id (issue
    #41: codex reports none of its own), cost_usd always null (issue
    #41's auth addendum)."""
    tokens_input, tokens_output = usage_fields(events)
    return {
        "model": model,
        "model_version": None,
        "adapter_version": adapter_version(),
        "tokens": {"input": tokens_input, "output": tokens_output},
        "cost_usd": None,
    }


def main() -> int:
    config = load_config()
    stage = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_STAGE", "build")
    verifying = stage == "verify"
    counseling = stage == "counsel"
    if verifying:
        instruction, schema = verify_instruction(), VERIFY_SCHEMA
    elif counseling:
        instruction, schema = counsel_instruction(), COUNSEL_SCHEMA
    else:
        instruction, schema = build_instruction(), STATUS_SCHEMA

    model = config.get("model")
    if not model:
        sys.stderr.write(
            "adapters/codex/run: refused: [executor.codex].model is "
            "required -- no model id appears anywhere codex itself "
            "reports (the event stream, codex doctor, or any config file)\n")
        return 1

    ceiling = ceiling_refusal(instruction)
    if ceiling:
        sys.stderr.write(f"adapters/codex/run: refused: {ceiling}\n")
        # Reported, not left for the product to find in the words: nothing
        # was invoked, so no adapter version and no tokens.
        print(json.dumps({"model": model, "model_version": None, "adapter_version": None,
                          "tokens": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "cost_usd": None,
                          "class": "ceiling", "detail": ceiling}))
        return 1

    if os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ADAPTER_DRY_RUN"):
        argv = build_argv(Path("<schema.json>"), Path("<output.json>"), config, stage)
        print(json.dumps({"argv": argv, "instruction": instruction, "schema": schema}))
        return 0

    if not os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE"):
        auth_error = check_auth(config)
        if auth_error:
            sys.stderr.write(f"adapters/codex/run: refused: {auth_error}\n")
            return 1

    responses = load_canned_responses()
    events_path = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_EVENTS")

    raw, events, final, exit_code = invoke(instruction, schema, config, stage, responses, 0)
    # keep the evidence: both channels, not just the fields below, are
    # the transcript build-NN.log saves -- a halt otherwise cannot be
    # explained after the fact. final is printed here too (issue #88):
    # a question the executor answers on the first call otherwise never
    # reaches the transcript at all, since only the terminal report line
    # -- printed once, at the very end -- carried it before.
    print(raw.rstrip("\n") if raw.strip() else "(codex produced no output)")
    if final is not None:
        print(json.dumps(final))
    if events_path:
        write_events(events_path, events)

    if exit_code != 0:
        detail = transient_detail(events)
        if detail:
            line = {**report_fields(model, events), "class": "transient", "detail": detail}
            print(json.dumps(line))
        return exit_code

    # An absent or unparseable output file is a failure whether this is
    # a build or a verify -- the same class as claude-code's absent
    # structured_output.
    if final is None:
        return 1

    if verifying:
        common = report_fields(model, events)
        line = {**common,
               "findings": final.get("findings"),
               "tests_named": final.get("tests_named")}
        print(json.dumps(line))
        return 0

    if counseling:
        common = report_fields(model, events)
        line = {**common,
               "class": final.get("class"),
               "verdict": final.get("verdict"),
               "citations": final.get("citations"),
               "proposal": final.get("proposal")}
        print(json.dumps(line))
        return 0

    status = final.get("status", "done")
    question = final.get("question")
    cls = question_class(status, question)

    if cls == "fail":
        return 1

    if cls == "report":
        # Issue #88, class A: a report in the question channel is
        # re-instructed once, in this same invocation, before it ever
        # reaches a person.
        reinstruction = instruction + "\n\n" + REINSTRUCTION
        raw2, events2, final2, exit_code2 = invoke(
            reinstruction, schema, config, stage, responses, 1)
        print(raw2.rstrip("\n") if raw2.strip() else "(codex produced no output)")
        if final2 is not None:
            print(json.dumps(final2))
        if events_path:
            write_events(events_path, events2)

        if exit_code2 != 0:
            return exit_code2
        if final2 is None:
            return 1

        status2 = final2.get("status", "done")
        question2 = final2.get("question")
        cls2 = question_class(status2, question2)
        if cls2 == "fail":
            return 1

        common2 = report_fields(model, events2)
        # A non-question costs two executor turns, not one: sum both
        # calls' tokens into the one report line this invocation gets,
        # whatever the retry's own outcome. cost_usd stays null (issue
        # #41): null plus null is still null.
        tokens1_input, tokens1_output = usage_fields(events)
        common2["tokens"]["input"] += tokens1_input
        common2["tokens"]["output"] += tokens1_output

        if cls2 == "report":
            # Two reports in a row: still a halt (someone must look),
            # but the transcript above already carries both raw
            # results, and this report line names the class so a
            # reader -- or a future record field, not this one -- can
            # tell it apart from a genuine question.
            print("adapters/codex/run: halted: class non-question -- the "
                  "executor reported instead of asking, twice in a row")
            line = {**common2, "status": "question", "question": question2,
                   "class": "non-question"}
            print(json.dumps(line))
            return 0

        line = {**common2, "status": status2, "question": question2}
        print(json.dumps(line))
        return 0

    common = report_fields(model, events)
    line = {**common, "status": status, "question": question}
    print(json.dumps(line))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
